# apps/console — build-time configuration and baked runtime defaults.
#
# Read by `next build`, both locally and inside apps/console/Dockerfile. Next
# loads `.env.production` before it evaluates next.config.mjs, so the first
# sections take effect at build time: the NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined
# literally into the client bundle (a computed lookup such as
# `process.env[name]` would NOT be replaced — see src/lib/config/site.ts), and
# ENABLE_HSTS fixes the response headers the built server sends. Changing a
# build-time value requires a rebuild, not a restart.
#
# The real process environment still wins over this file (@next/env only fills
# in keys that are absent), which is why apps/console/Dockerfile declares no
# ARG/ENV for these build-time variables: an unpassed --build-arg becomes an
# empty string, counts as "defined", and would shadow everything below.
#
# The last section is different in kind: the standalone server loads this same
# file again at boot, so the GitHub egress proxy endpoints there are runtime
# defaults rather than build inputs. In the image they are baked at build time
# — change them with `docker run -e ...`, which outranks the file and needs no
# rebuild — or, for a bare `next start` checkout, edit this file or
# `.env.production.local`. Public values only throughout: this file is tracked
# in git and copied verbatim into `.next/standalone`. Secrets
# (BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, CONSOLE_DATABASE_URL, EGRESS_SECRET, ...) hold no value
# here: they come from `docker run -e ...` or from `.env.production.local`,
# which git ignores. Every variable is documented in .env.example.

# ===========================================================================
# Site identity
# ===========================================================================
#
# The name/description values mirror the fallbacks in src/lib/config/site.ts.
# Because they are set here, those fallbacks are inert in a production build:
# renaming the product means editing this file (and site.ts too, so an
# unconfigured build stays coherent).
#
# Canonical origin. It is `metadataBase`, so every relative URL — canonical,
# OG image, sitemap entry — resolves against it: absolute, scheme included, no
# path, no trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_ORIGIN=https://www.vercelai.cn
# Host alone, printed next to the wordmark.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_HOST=vercelai.cn
# Chinese display name and the `og:site_name` value.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME=VercelAI雷达
# English display name, for `lang="en"` surfaces.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME_EN=VercelAI Radar
# One-line pitch: footer blurb and OG description.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_TAGLINE=记录 stargazer 的到达时间，判断开源项目正在变好还是变坏。
# One paragraph: meta[name=description], the JSON-LD WebSite node, /llms.txt.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_DESCRIPTION=VercelAI雷达记录每个 stargazer 到达 GitHub 的时间，据此判断一个开源项目正在变好还是变坏。周榜、月榜、年度飙升榜与异动流都以原始星标增量、相对增速和维护停滞证据对外提供，每条结论都能点开复核。
# The one repository the footer links to and the only `sameAs` the Organization
# node declares.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_GITHUB_URL=https://github.com/openroute-ai/openmcp
# The long-form documentation host — a different deployment (the docs app),
# linked out to rather than part of this origin. Must be an absolute origin:
# site.ts rejects anything unparseable (a relative `/docs` would be silently
# dropped in favour of the default), so the effective value is written out
# explicitly here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_DOCS_ORIGIN=https://www.vercelai.cn/docs

# ===========================================================================
# Response headers
# ===========================================================================
#
# `ENABLE_HSTS=1` sends `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000;
# includeSubDomains` on every response. Read once, when next.config.mjs loads
# during the build, so the choice is frozen into the built server.
#
# Deliberately empty (= off) in this file: an HSTS header is a browser-side
# promise for a whole year, and a deployment still reachable only over plain
# http becomes unreachable for every browser that ever saw it. Set it to `1`
# here — and rebuild — once this origin really serves TLS, directly or behind a
# proxy that forwards `X-Forwarded-Proto: https`.
ENABLE_HSTS=

# ===========================================================================
# Analytics (optional)
# ===========================================================================
#
# `<Analytics />` in the locale layout loads each provider only when its
# variable(s) are set, and only in a production build — commented out is the
# supported "off" state, not a placeholder. Umami, Plausible and DataFast each
# need their variables together; Ahrefs and Seline are single tokens. Providers
# come from @workspace/shared-next/analytics.
#
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=4b2d5f75-3c44-4fb2-a279-40cd1134d20d
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_SCRIPT=https://umami.julianshuke.top/script.js
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_SCRIPT=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DATAFAST_DOMAIN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DATAFAST_WEBSITE_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AHREFS_WEBSITE_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SELINE_TOKEN=

# ===========================================================================
# Runtime — GitHub egress proxy (apps/vercel-egress, deployed on Vercel)
# ===========================================================================
#
# Not a build input: src/lib/env.ts reads these per call (sync, cron, scan), so
# they never enter the client bundle. When GitHub is unreachable from where
# console runs, all GitHub traffic — data sync and sandbox scan — leaves through
# the Hono proxy in apps/vercel-egress; the GitHub token then lives only on the
# proxy, and this app holds just the shared secret. Reference: .env.example;
# deployment steps: docs/design/design-vercel-egress-proxy-and-sandbox.md.
#
# The URLs below are placeholders on purpose: env.ts parses them with
# `new URL`, and `https://<proxy-domain>` does not parse, so they read as unset
# and the console keeps talking straight to api.github.com (which then requires
# GITHUB_ACCESS_TOKEN at run time). Replace them with the deployed proxy origin
# (e.g. https://<project>-xxxx.vercel.app from Settings → Domains).
#
# Egress switches on only when EGRESS_BASE_URL and EGRESS_SECRET are BOTH set
# (env.ts `egressConfigured`): a base URL alone still goes direct, and the
# secret is what authenticates every forwarded request (`x-egress-secret`) —
# including the scan forward to `EGRESS_BASE_URL + /api/scan`.
GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=https://<proxy-domain>/api/github/rest
GITHUB_GRAPHQL_URL=https://<proxy-domain>/api/github/graphql
EGRESS_BASE_URL=https://<proxy-domain>
# The shared secret — deliberately empty, never a value in this file (it is
# committed and baked into the image). Supply it at run time: `docker run -e
# EGRESS_SECRET=...`, or `.env.production.local` for a bare `next start`.
# Empty keeps egress off, which is the supported default; URL + secret together
# are what turn it on.
EGRESS_SECRET=

